-- #1161 SYMPTOM 2, the half F-3a-ii did NOT fix: a declared `=>` constraint that
-- NOTHING in the program satisfies is ACCEPTED at exit 0, and the call silently
-- dispatches to an unrelated impl.
--
-- THE CORRECT BEHAVIOUR IS A REJECTION.  `useIx : Ix a Bool => a -> Int` is
-- called at `a = Int`, so the goal is the predicate `Ix Int Bool`.  There is no
-- `impl Ix Int Bool` anywhere; DICT-SEMANTICS §3 makes `match(IE, Ix Int Bool)`
-- EMPTY, `inst` inapplicable, and the goal a missing-instance rejection
-- (T-NO-IMPL).  The `impl … requires` twin of this exact shape IS correctly
-- rejected today ("No impl of Ix for Int Bool"), which is what localises the
-- defect to this channel.
--
-- This file reproduces the BUG: `check --json` reports {"diagnostics":[]} at
-- exit 0 and `run` prints 222 -- the body of the ONLY declared impl, `Ix Int
-- Char`, which the goal does not match on either argument pair.
--
-- ⚠️ CORROBORATING, SAME ROOT, NOT SEPARATELY PINNED: `medaka check` prints the
-- inferred scheme as `useIx : a -> Int`, with the `Ix a Bool =>` context DROPPED.
-- Both observations come from `declaredSchemeOblsFor` -> `declaredOblOne` ->
-- `constraintArgMonos`, which returns `None` for any constraint argument that is
-- not a bare type variable, on an obligation payload that is IDS ONLY and has no
-- room for a concrete argument.  No obligation is recorded, so nothing is checked
-- and nothing is displayed.
--
-- ⚠️ WHAT IS ALREADY FIXED, AND MUST NOT BE RE-PINNED HERE: #1161 SYMPTOM 1 --
-- declaration order deciding which impl a SATISFIABLE multi-parameter `=>`
-- constraint dispatches to.  F-3a-ii closed that by recording the predicate's
-- whole argument vector beside `funConstraintsRef` and routing on it; it is
-- graded positively at test/dict_fixtures/s3-nary-sig-constraint-goal-vector.mdk
-- (value + declaration-order permutation + emitted arity).  Widening THAT
-- routing goal was measured NOT to fix this file: making `recordCallObligations`
-- n-ary from the same vector still accepts this program at exit 0, because the
-- acceptance decision is made upstream on the obligation channel.  Closing this
-- is a separate obligation-payload change.
interface Ix a b where
  ix : a -> b -> Int

impl Ix Int Char where
  ix _ _ = 222

useIx : Ix a Bool => a -> Int
useIx x = ix x True

main = println (useIx 1)
